          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The height above the ground, in metres, of a person’s eyes varies directly with the square of the distance, in kilometres, that the person can see to the horizon.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p39-q14-given",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A person whose eyes are 1.6 m above the ground can see 4.5 km out to sea.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p39-q14-request",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "How high above the ground, in metres, would a person’s eyes need to be to see an island that is 15 km out to sea? Give your answer correct to one decimal place.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$17.8\\text{ m}$",
      "worked": "Let $h$ be eye height in metres and $d$ be distance to the horizon in kilometres.\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=kd^2\\\\1.6&=k(4.5)^2\\\\k&=\\frac{1.6}{4.5^2}\\\\h&=\\frac{1.6}{4.5^2}(15)^2\\\\h&=\\frac{160}{9}\\text{ m}\\\\h&\\approx17.8\\text{ m}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
